---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import HEICToPDFSize from '../components/HEICToPDFSize.astro';
import SEOContentHEIC from '../components/SEOContentHEIC.astro';

const SEO_TITLE = "Convert HEIC to PDF — Free Local Image Converter";
const SEO_DESC = "Convert Apple iPhone HEIC and HEIF photos directly to PDF document format. 100% free, completely secure, and processed entirely offline on your device.";
---

<Layout title={SEO_TITLE} description={SEO_DESC}>
  <style is:global>
    :root {
      --color-lime: #f43f5e; /* PDF suite color */
    }
  </style>

  <section class="relative z-20 pt-20 container-stripe">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; background: rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.2); border-radius: 100px; padding: 5px 14px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--color-lime);">Zero-Server Converter</span>
      </div>

      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 4.5rem); line-height: 1; margin: 0 0 24px; letter-spacing: -0.05em;">
        Convert HEIC <br/>
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">to PDF.</span>
      </h1>
      <p style="color: var(--color-ink-2); font-size: 1.125rem; margin-bottom: 24px; max-width: 500px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        Instantly transform your iPhone photos into a PDF document for portals. Processed 100% securely on your local device.
      </p>
    </div>

    <HEICToPDFSize />
  </section>

  <SEOContentHEIC toolName="convert-heic-to-pdf" />

  <!-- Unique In-Depth Technical Guide & SEO Article -->
  <article class="heic-pdf-guide" style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 80px auto 0; padding: 0 24px 80px; text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-body); line-height: 1.8; color: var(--color-ink-2);">
    
    <div style="height: 1px; background: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, var(--color-border), transparent); margin-bottom: 64px;"></div>

    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 2.25rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.03em; margin-bottom: 24px; color: var(--color-ink); line-height: 1.2;">
      The Ultimate Guide to Converting Apple HEIC Photos Directly to PDF Documents
    </h2>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Capturing high-resolution photographs with an Apple iPhone has become the standard method for digitizing physical paperwork, academic transcripts, legal contracts, property blueprints, and government identification cards. Ever since Apple introduced iOS 11, iPhones have defaulted to storing still captures in the <strong>High Efficiency Image Container (HEIC)</strong> format based on modern HEVC (H.265) compression. While HEIC is an outstanding format for conserving internal smartphone storage—slashing raw photo storage requirements by nearly 50% compared to legacy JPEGs while retaining 10-bit color data—it poses severe compatibility headaches when dealing with official portals.
    </p>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Whether you are submitting educational certificates on the <strong>Union Public Service Commission (UPSC)</strong> portal, uploading caste and domicile certificates for <strong>NTA NEET</strong> or <strong>JEE Main</strong> counselling, submitting identity proofs to an <strong>EPFO</strong> or <strong>MCA21</strong> portal, or filing a legal affidavit on an <strong>eCourts India</strong> judicial portal, the acceptable file format is almost universally a single <strong>PDF document</strong>. Uploading raw <code>.heic</code> or <code>.heif</code> files triggers immediate server rejection errors. Our zero-server HEIC to PDF converter solves this friction instantly, allowing you to transform your iPhone photos directly into clean, standardized A4 PDF files right inside your browser with complete privacy.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      1. Converting iPhone Photos Directly to PDF: Why You Should Skip the Intermediate JPG Step
    </h3>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      The traditional workaround used by millions of iPhone owners is unnecessarily tedious and technically flawed: users first convert their HEIC photo to a JPEG file using an image app or desktop software, save the new JPEG to their camera roll or download folder, and then upload that JPEG into a secondary JPEG-to-PDF tool. This two-phase conversion workflow is not just slow; it causes severe <em>generation loss</em> and compromises visual quality.
    </p>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Every time an image is converted to JPEG, it undergoes lossy Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) block quantization. Converting an already-compressed HEIC file into a temporary JPEG compresses the image data a second time, introducing noticeable compression artifacts, mosquito noise around high-contrast edges, and visible blurring across fine text, official seals, and candidate signatures. When that re-encoded JPEG is wrapped into a PDF document, it can suffer yet another layer of re-sampling.
    </p>

    <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-lime); border-radius: 0 16px 16px 0; padding: 24px; margin: 32px 0;">
      <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 8px;">
        The PdfAndImg Direct WebAssembly Pipeline
      </h4>
      <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2);">
        PdfAndImg uses a high-performance in-memory WebAssembly (Wasm) architecture. When you select your HEIC files, our browser engine reads the raw HEVC bitstream directly into volatile RAM, decodes the native color matrices, and compiles the raster bitmaps directly onto a vector-scaled PDF document coordinate grid. By eliminating intermediate temporary files, you preserve pristine document sharpness, save local storage space, and complete the conversion in a fraction of a second.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      2. Essential Real-World Use Cases for iPhone HEIC to PDF Conversion
    </h3>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Converting camera snapshots into standardized PDFs is essential across multiple professional, educational, and personal workflows in India and internationally:
    </p>

    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 32px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: bold; flex-shrink: 0; margin-top: 2px;">▸</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Document Scanning with iPhone:</strong>
          Instead of downloading shady, ad-bloated mobile scanner apps that slap ugly watermarks across your documents, you can snap crisp photos of physical Marksheets (10th, 12th, Degree), University Transcripts, Caste Certificates, Non-Creamy Layer (OBC-NCL) Certificates, EWS certificates, or Aadhaar and PAN cards. Converting them directly to PDF creates a clean, authoritative submission package accepted by IBPS, SSC, State PSCs, and corporate HR departments.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: bold; flex-shrink: 0; margin-top: 2px;">▸</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Consolidated Medical & Insurance Claims:</strong>
          Third-Party Administrators (TPAs) such as MediBuddy, Star Health, Vidal Health, and Care Health Insurance require all hospital discharge summaries, diagnostic laboratory reports, and pharmacy bills to be combined into a single chronological PDF. Snapping your receipts on an iPhone and merging them here creates an organized dossier that speeds up claim settlement.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: bold; flex-shrink: 0; margin-top: 2px;">▸</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Creating Professional Photo Albums & Visual Portfolios:</strong>
          Architects, interior designers, civil contractors, and wedding photographers can assemble 10 to 50 curated high-resolution iPhone photos into an elegant, borderless A4 PDF photobook. PDF portfolios look uniform across all screen sizes and can be easily forwarded via WhatsApp or email to clients without the hassle of unzipping image folders.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: bold; flex-shrink: 0; margin-top: 2px;">▸</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Legal Affidavits & Court E-Filings:</strong>
          Under the eCourts project governing District Courts, High Courts, and the Supreme Court of India, judicial filings require strict A4 portrait PDF submissions with proper margin clearances for court stamps and digital signing tokens.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Section 3 -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      3. Understanding the Impact: How iPhone Camera Quality Affects PDF File Size
    </h3>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Modern iPhones feature remarkable camera hardware. While older models (iPhone 8 through iPhone 13) capture at <strong>12 Megapixels (4032 x 3024 pixels)</strong>, newer flagship models like the iPhone 14 Pro, iPhone 15 Pro, and iPhone 16 Pro utilize <strong>48 Megapixel (8064 x 6048 pixels)</strong> Quad-Bayer sensors. This massive resolution creates a distinct challenge when producing PDF files:
    </p>

    <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); padding: 28px; border-radius: 16px; margin: 28px 0; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
      <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 16px;">
        The "Bloated PDF" Dilemma: Raw Memory vs. Print Requirements
      </h4>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 24px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));">
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-lime); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 0.9375rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">Memory Expansion</strong>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6;">
            While an on-disk HEIC photo might only weigh 3 MB to 5 MB due to Apple's HEVC intra-frame encoding, unpacking that 48 MP image into an uncompressed raster bitmap requires <code>8064 x 6048 x 3 bytes</code> = <strong>over 146 Megabytes of raw RAM</strong>. Unoptimized converters dump raw pixels straight into PDF streams, creating 50 MB+ documents that crash portal upload limits.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-lime); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 0.9375rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">Physical Print Reality (DPI)</strong>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6;">
            An international standard A4 sheet (210 x 297 mm) printed at commercial magazine-quality <strong>300 DPI</strong> only requires <strong>2480 x 3508 pixels (~8.7 MP)</strong>. At standard 150 DPI document clarity, an A4 page only needs <strong>1240 x 1754 pixels (~2.1 MP)</strong>. Embedding raw 48 MP photos into a PDF provides zero perceptible clarity improvement while wasting bandwidth.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      PdfAndImg's local conversion algorithm automatically balances visual quality and file density. By applying high-fidelity re-compression (0.90 quality factor) mapped to standard A4 page dimensions, your converted PDF retains pin-sharp legibility on fine printed text, official watermarks, and security holograms, while keeping average file size between <strong>350 KB and 1.2 MB per page</strong>—comfortably below the 2 MB to 5 MB ceilings enforced by recruitment portals.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 4 -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      4. Merging Multiple HEIC Photos into a Single Unified PDF
    </h3>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Government and corporate application portals in India strictly forbid uploading multiple loose image files or compressed <code>.zip</code> archives. For instance, when submitting semester marksheets for a 4-year engineering degree or a 3-year B.Com / B.Sc degree, candidate portals provide exactly <em>one file upload button</em> titled "Consolidated Academic Transcript (Single PDF)".
    </p>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Our tool allows you to select <strong>up to 50 HEIC or HEIF files simultaneously</strong> and merge them into a single, cohesive multi-page PDF document. The tool processes your images in sequential order:
    </p>

    <ol style="padding-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 32px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;">
      <li><strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Sequential Batch Ingestion:</strong> Drag and drop your photos in the order you wish them to appear. The tool loads them into a temporary memory queue.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Orientation Selection:</strong> Choose <strong>Portrait</strong> for standard vertical documents, letters, marksheets, and official circulars, or <strong>Landscape</strong> for wide certificates, panoramic project shots, or landscape spreadsheets.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Smart Margin Adjustments:</strong> Select between three margin presets:
        <ul style="list-style: disc; padding-left: 24px; margin-top: 8px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px;">
          <li><strong>No Margin (0 mm):</strong> Maximizes image area to the exact page edge—ideal for photographic albums and full-bleed image portfolios.</li>
          <li><strong>Small Margin (10 mm):</strong> Adds a clean 10mm white border around each photo, providing a standard presentation suitable for office printing.</li>
          <li><strong>Large Margin (20 mm):</strong> Adds a generous 20mm margin around the image, ensuring crucial document text is never obscured by hole punches, staplers, or spiral binding combs.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">Proportional Scaling:</strong> The conversion engine automatically calculates image aspect ratios against the A4 canvas, ensuring photos are centered symmetrically without stretching or clipping content.</li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      5. In-Depth Comparison: PdfAndImg vs. Apple "Print to PDF" vs. Cloud Converters
    </h3>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Many iOS users rely on Apple's built-in "Print to PDF" secret gesture (Photos app → Share → Print → Pinch-Out on preview → Share → Save to Files), while others use remote cloud converters like ILovePDF or Smallpdf. Here is how PdfAndImg compares across key technical and privacy parameters:
    </p>

    <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 32px 0; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px;">
      <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="background: var(--color-surface-2); color: var(--color-ink); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-mid);">
            <th style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 700;">Feature / Capability</th>
            <th style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime);">PdfAndImg (Zero-Server)</th>
            <th style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 700;">Apple "Print to PDF"</th>
            <th style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 700;">Online Cloud Converters</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Data Privacy & Security</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: var(--color-lime);">100% Client-Side (No Uploads)</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Local Device Only</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: #ef4444;">Files Uploaded to Cloud Server</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: rgba(255,255,255,0.015);">
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Cross-Platform Availability</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: var(--color-lime);">Any Device (iOS, Windows, Mac, Linux)</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: #ef4444;">Apple iOS / macOS Only</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Any Device with Web Access</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Margin Control</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: var(--color-lime);">Customizable (0mm, 10mm, 20mm)</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: #ef4444;">Fixed Printer Margins Only</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Limited (Usually Paywalled)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: rgba(255,255,255,0.015);">
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Resulting PDF File Size</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: var(--color-lime);">Optimized for Portals (350KB-1.2MB/page)</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: #ef4444;">Often Bloated (5MB-15MB/page)</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Variable (Depends on Tier)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Batch Conversion Limits</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: var(--color-lime);">Up to 50 Images per Batch Free</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Photos App Selection (Clunky)</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: #ef4444;">Strict Limits (e.g. 2-3 files/hr free)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Account / Subscription</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: var(--color-lime);">Zero Signup, 100% Free Forever</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Included with iOS</td>
            <td style="padding: 14px 18px; color: #ef4444;">Constant Upsells & Paywalls</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 6 -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      6. A4 vs. US Letter Page Sizing: Standards, Dimensions & Printing Risks
    </h3>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      When converting digital photos into document format, page geometry matters profoundly. The two globally dominant document paper standards are <strong>ISO 216 A4</strong> and <strong>North American US Letter</strong>. Understanding the difference between them is vital to avoid truncated text or rejection when your PDF is printed or evaluated.
    </p>

    <div style="display: grid; gap: 24px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); margin: 32px 0;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">■</span> ISO 216 A4 Paper
        </h4>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          <li><strong>Dimensions:</strong> 210 mm x 297 mm (8.27 in x 11.69 in)</li>
          <li><strong>Aspect Ratio:</strong> 1:1.414 (1:√2, the Lichtenberg ratio)</li>
          <li><strong>Standard Adoption:</strong> Mandated in India, United Kingdom, Europe, Japan, Australia, and over 140 nations.</li>
          <li><strong>Official Usage:</strong> Indian High Courts, UPSC, SSC, IBPS, universities, and commercial contracts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="color: var(--color-ink-3);">■</span> ANSI US Letter Paper
        </h4>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          <li><strong>Dimensions:</strong> 215.9 mm x 279.4 mm (8.50 in x 11.00 in)</li>
          <li><strong>Aspect Ratio:</strong> 1:1.294</li>
          <li><strong>Standard Adoption:</strong> Exclusively standard in the United States, Canada, and parts of Mexico.</li>
          <li><strong>Official Usage:</strong> US federal filings, North American corporate templates, and US visa paperwork.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      <strong>The Printing Mismatch Trap:</strong> Notice that A4 is significantly taller (297 mm vs 279.4 mm) but slightly narrower (210 mm vs 215.9 mm) than US Letter. If you generate a PDF in US Letter format and attempt to print it in India or Europe where laser printers are pre-loaded with A4 paper trays, two critical errors occur:
    </p>

    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 32px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;">
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;">
        <span style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: bold;">✕</span>
        <span><strong>Automatic Scaling Down:</strong> Printer drivers default to "Fit to Printable Area", reducing the entire document by 6% to 9%. This shrinks signature lines and reduces tiny serial numbers or examination marks below legal legibility standards.</span>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;">
        <span style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: bold;">✕</span>
        <span><strong>Margin Truncation:</strong> If printed at 100% scale without auto-shrinking, the printer cuts off the bottom or top 17.6 millimeters of the page, slicing off official date stamps, page numbers, or counter-signatures.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      PdfAndImg defaults to the exact ISO A4 standard (210 x 297 mm) with millimeter-precise image centering. Whether your PDF is reviewed digitally on a laptop or printed out at a physical document verification counter, the margins remain impeccably aligned.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 7: FAQs -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 24px; color: var(--color-ink);">
      Frequently Asked Questions About Converting HEIC to PDF
    </h3>

    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 24px;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 8px;">
          Why does my iPhone take pictures in HEIC format instead of standard PDF or JPG?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2);">
          Apple set HEIC (High Efficiency Image Container) as the default photo format starting with iOS 11 to save storage space on iPhones. HEIC utilizes modern HEVC video compression algorithms to capture rich 10-bit images that consume roughly half the storage space of a 24-bit JPEG. However, because HEIC is an image container rather than a document container, official portals cannot accept it as a document format like PDF.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 8px;">
          Can I convert multiple HEIC photos into a single PDF document on my iPhone?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2);">
          Yes! Open this page in Safari or Chrome on your iPhone, tap "Browse files", tap "Photo Library", and select up to 50 photos. Our converter will process all photos in order and merge them into a single, multi-page A4 PDF file that you can immediately download directly to your iOS "Files" app or share to Google Drive and WhatsApp.
        </p>
